{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And it will not be able to see the earth.</b> Rashi explained: 'it' means the one who sees. And the primary word is missing from the text [i.e., the subject 'the locust' is elided]. And there is further difficulty with his interpretation, for the fact that they would not be able to see the earth makes no difference with regard to the damage; and at the very least he should have put the statement 'and it shall eat the remainder of what was left' first, for that is the essence of the damage. And so too in the account of the plague's actual infliction it is said likewise (Exodus 10:15), 'and the land was darkened and it ate all the grass of the land' — hear from this that the reason for the eating was the darkening of the land, and the matter requires explanation." "And I say: from the well-known fact that all blind people eat and are never satisfied — so too this locust: through their multitude causing the land to be darkened, the locust too cannot see what it is eating, and it is like a blind person eating at a trough and never sated. And therefore it shall eat all that is in the field (Exodus 10:5), and still it will not be sated until it enters from the field also into the houses, 'and they shall fill your houses' etc. (ibid.). And this is what it says: 'and it shall not be able to see' — that is, the locust that was mentioned earlier shall not be able to see the earth. And because the locust does not see the earth, it will eat everything and not be sated, until afterward it seeks also what is in the houses. And what we find — that in the actual infliction of the plague they ate only what was in the fields, and we do not find that they entered the houses — is because it says 'and Pharaoh hastened to call for Moses and Aaron' (Exodus 10:16): why is the expression 'and he hastened' not said in connection with any other plague? But rather because Pharaoh knew that if they would not find enough in the fields they would eat also what was in the houses, therefore 'Pharaoh hastened' before they could enter his house. And therefore 'and now, please forgive my sin' (ibid. 10:17) — and now, immediately and at once, before they enter the houses."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:53.501190903Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}